content.text:
verdict: findings
checks:
  - {claim: "Summary 1", question: "Do SFIA 9, IASA, Open CA and every major cloud vendor define a solutions-architect role, and do TOGAF and Gartner scope it to a single project/release?", source_reopened: "https://sfia-online.org/en/sfia-9/skills/solution-architecture ; https://www.opengroup.org/certifications/certified-architect-open-ca ; https://cloud.google.com/learn/certification/cloud-architect ; https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Solution_architecture", result: fail, notes: "SFIA/Open CA/IASA confirmed; but Google's title is 'Cloud Architect' (per E11), so 'every major cloud vendor' overreaches; and Gartner (via Wikipedia) says only 'an architectural description of a specific solution' — the 'single project or project release' scope is TOGAF-via-Berrisford, not Gartner."}
  - {claim: "Summary 2", question: "Do Open CA, IASA and SFIA say the quoted phrases?", source_reopened: "https://www.opengroup.org/certifications/certified-architect-open-ca ; https://iasa-global.github.io/btabok/job_description.html ; https://sfia-online.org/en/sfia-9/skills/solution-architecture", result: pass, notes: "All three quotes found verbatim."}
  - {claim: "Summary 3", question: "Does QLD say 'decides which technologies'; does IASA place tool selection with the enterprise team; does Hohpe say 'within agreed-upon bounds…'?", source_reopened: "https://www.forgov.qld.gov.au/recruitment-performance-and-career/career-development/develop-digital-and-ict-capabilities/skills-framework-for-the-information-age-sfia-role-profiles/solutions-architect ; https://iasa-global.github.io/btabok/job_description.html ; https://martinfowler.com/articles/architect-elevator.html", result: pass, notes: "QLD: 'Solutions architects decide which technologies to use… They are the link between the needs of the organisation and the developers.' IASA actual text: 'The IT tool selection for a project has to be given by the Global Enterprise architect team not the BU IT teams' (draft renders 'must be given… not the business unit IT teams' — smoothed, not verbatim). Hohpe quote confirmed on the Fowler-hosted page only; the EIP page reopened does not contain it. Reconciliation correctly labelled as judgment."}
  - {claim: "Summary 4", question: "Does the DDD source say boundaries can't be delegated, and does SAFe say 'define the systems, subsystems and interfaces'?", source_reopened: "https://www.avanscoperta.it/en/context-mapping/ ; https://scrum-master.org/en/role-of-the-sae-or-system-architect-in-an-safe-train/", result: pass, notes: "Avanscoperta verbatim. The 'systems, subsystems and interfaces' line is scrum-master.org (secondary), which the summary attributes to 'SAFe's architect' without the secondhand marker F3 carries."}
  - {claim: "Summary 5", question: "Does Pettersson state the decision-rights lists and the 'unclear decision rights' line; does SAFe frame architect and PM as peer leaders?", source_reopened: "https://pettersson.dev/governance/architect-productowner/ ; https://framework.scaledagile.com/product-management/", result: pass, notes: "All seven-item lists and both quotes verbatim; SAFe PM page: 'works closely with the System Architect, Release Train Engineer, and Business Owners as leaders of the Agile Release Train (ART).'"}
  - {claim: "Summary 6", question: "Does Fowler say the 'inversely proportional' line; are ADRs Adopt in source control; is the ARB line verbatim; does TOGAF Phase G give enforce/relief/change?", source_reopened: "https://martinfowler.com/ieeeSoftware/whoNeedsArchitect.pdf (PDF not text-extractable in this pass; confirmed via https://weekly-geekly.imtqy.com/articles/350176/index.html) ; https://www.thoughtworks.com/radar/techniques/lightweight-architecture-decision-records ; https://www.thoughtworks.com/en-us/radar/techniques/architecture-advice-process ; https://conexiam.com/togaf-adm-phase-g-ensure-value-with-implementation-governance/", result: pass, notes: "Radar: Adopt, 'storing these details in source control'; Trial, 'counterproductive, often hindering workflow and correlating with low organizational performance'. Conexiam does not contain the sentence 'enforce the target architecture, grant temporary relief, or change the architecture itself' verbatim — it reads 'Enforcing the target means… Granting temporary relief means… Changing the architecture means…'; the draft's quotation marks should become a paraphrase."}
  - {claim: "F1", question: "Do the SFIA level descriptors, Open CA, IASA deliverables, and the TOGAF/Wikipedia/SAFe scope statements read as quoted?", source_reopened: "https://sfia-online.org/en/sfia-9/skills/solution-architecture ; https://iasa-global.github.io/btabok/job_description.html ; http://grahamberrisford.com/01EAingeneral/EAroles/EA%20and%20SA%20roles%20in%20TOGAF.htm ; https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Solution_architecture ; https://framework.scaledagile.com/solution-architect", result: fail, notes: "SFIA L5/L6 lead lines verbatim, but L6 reads 'Manages trade-offs and balances functional, service quality, cost efficiency and systems management requirements', not 'manages trade-offs across functional and cost requirements'. Wikipedia reads 'The Open Group does not recognize the role \"solution architect\" in its TOGAF skills framework' (E5 misquotes it). IASA deliverables 'solution designs' and 'impact analyses on technical strategy' not found as an enumerated list — only 'technical design documentation' and 'formal models of the system'; mark that clause UNVERIFIED. Berrisford and SAFe Solution Architect abstract confirmed."}
  - {claim: "F2", question: "Are the QLD, AWS, Microsoft, IASA, Hohpe and Harmel-Law quotes verbatim and correctly placed?", source_reopened: "QLD (URL above) ; https://docs.aws.amazon.com/aws-certification/latest/solutions-architect-associate-03/solutions-architect-associate-03.html ; https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/credentials/certifications/azure-solutions-architect/ ; https://iasa-global.github.io/btabok/job_description.html ; https://martinfowler.com/articles/scaling-architecture-conversationally.html", result: pass, notes: "QLD, AWS, Microsoft, Harmel-Law verbatim (QLD skills ARCH/CNSL/TECH/EMRG all level 5 confirmed). IASA line is in the Enterprise Architect section, not a solutions-architect description — draft's placement ('IASA places… with the enterprise architecture team') is consistent, but the wording is not verbatim (see Summary 3). The QLD URL in the draft's Sources is truncated/404; the real path is /recruitment-performance-and-career/career-development/develop-digital-and-ict-capabilities/skills-framework-for-the-information-age-sfia-role-profiles/solutions-architect."}
  - {claim: "F3", question: "Do Avanscoperta, scrum-master.org/SAFe, Mind the Product and continuous-architecture.org say what is quoted?", source_reopened: "https://www.avanscoperta.it/en/context-mapping/ ; https://www.avanscoperta.it/en/bounded-context/ ; https://framework.scaledagile.com/system-architect ; https://scrum-master.org/en/role-of-the-sae-or-system-architect-in-an-safe-train/ ; https://www.mindtheproduct.com/product-leaders-guide-to-team-topologies/ ; https://www.continuous-architecture.org/practices/team-topologies/", result: pass, notes: "All quotes verbatim; partnership is among the listed patterns. SAFe abstract confirms enablers/NFRs/capacity. scrum-master.org says 'in line with ART's business vision' — 'Product Management owns' is the draft's gloss, not the source's. Mind the Product: 'one of the most difficult but important responsibilities for product leaders'; continuous-architecture: 'after the architecture is defined' (draft's 'architecture defined' first is a fair paraphrase)."}
  - {claim: "F4", question: "Do Pettersson, ondata, SAFe, Lawson and Mind the Product say what is quoted?", source_reopened: "https://pettersson.dev/governance/architect-productowner/ ; https://ondata.blog/articles/on-the-usage-of-terms-product-owner-software-architect-and-product-manager/ ; https://framework.scaledagile.com/product-management/ ; https://www.linkedin.com/pulse/smell-single-backlog-service-based-architecture-james-lawson ; https://www.mindtheproduct.com/product-leaders-guide-to-team-topologies/", result: pass, notes: "All verbatim. ondata: 'define the product, starting from user requirements'; 'represents the client (user) voice'; 'defines how the product works internally, in other words draws the product architecture'; 'agree on the role description upfront'. Lawson: 'If you're creating or maintaining a service based architecture and you have a single backlog, you're setting yourself up for failure.'"}
  - {claim: "F5", question: "Do Fowler, the Radar entries, theartofcto and Conexiam say what is quoted?", source_reopened: "Fowler PDF unreadable (poppler absent; secondary confirmation only) ; Radar URLs above ; https://theartofcto.com/frameworks/architecture-review-process ; https://conexiam.com/togaf-adm-phase-g-ensure-value-with-implementation-governance/", result: fail, notes: "'Inversely proportional' confirmed as Fowler's own rule of thumb. 'The decisions that you wish you could get right early' is Fowler's rendering of Ralph Johnson's definition — attribute to Johnson via Fowler. theartofcto RACI: 'Propose architecture: Responsible Engineer, Accountable Engineer, Consulted ARB' confirmed, but the same table makes the CTO Accountable for Approve/Reject — 'even an ARB-centric RACI makes the proposing engineer Accountable' is selective. Conexiam enforce/relief/change line is not a verbatim quote (see Summary 6)."}
confidence_labels: "F4 'high' is not defensible: the decision-rights split rests on Pettersson and ondata (both secondary) with SAFe read only as an abstract; under the scheme that is medium, and the Limitations section itself concedes no three-way RACI was found. Summary 5 'High' inherits the same problem. F3 'high' is marginal — Avanscoperta is marked secondary in E16 and SAFe is abstract-only — but the two agree, so acceptable if Avanscoperta is treated as the community primary. No phrase combines confidence with likelihood."
top_changes:
  - "Summary 1: drop 'every major cloud vendor' (Google's role is 'Cloud Architect') and remove Gartner from the 'single project or release' scope claim — that scope is TOGAF via Berrisford; Gartner says only 'a specific solution'."
  - "Downgrade F4 and Summary 5 to medium: the decision-rights split is one practitioner secondary source (Pettersson) plus ondata, with SAFe abstract-only."
  - "Conexiam (F5, Summary 6): replace the quoted 'enforce the target architecture, grant temporary relief, or change the architecture itself' with a paraphrase; the source phrases them as three separate 'Enforcing… Granting… Changing…' sentences."
  - "F1: correct SFIA L6 to 'Manages trade-offs and balances functional, service quality, cost efficiency and systems management requirements'; correct the Wikipedia quote to 'does not recognize the role \"solution architect\" in its TOGAF skills framework'; mark IASA 'solution designs' and 'impact analyses on technical strategy' UNVERIFIED."
  - "F5: attribute 'decisions you wish you could get right early' to Ralph Johnson as reported by Fowler; qualify the theartofcto RACI note (CTO is Accountable for approve/reject in the same table)."
  - "F2/IASA: quote verbatim 'has to be given by the Global Enterprise architect team not the BU IT teams' and note it sits in the Enterprise Architect section."
  - "Sources: replace the truncated QLD URL with the full working path; note the Hohpe quote is on the martinfowler.com copy only; Summary 4 should mark the 'systems, subsystems and interfaces' line as scrum-master.org secondhand."
